THE PAPER. 28 pages. Quantitative boundary readout for primitives of constant forms on boxes.

Given a smooth (k-1)-form with exterior derivative equal to the standard volume form, how much of its boundary trace is forced by sharp or near-sharp saturation of the coefficient sup norm in the interior? On coordinate-monomial forms the paper proves the sharp bound with constant 1/(2k) for the midpoint-centred radial homotopy operator, and identifies the incidence obstruction to extending it to larger orientation classes. For a box with side lengths L_j it computes the extremal value as the reciprocal of twice the sum of the reciprocal side lengths, constructs an explicit affine minimizer, and proves a linear stability estimate controlling the boundary trace by the interior norm gap: every minimizer has the canonical trace on each codimension-one face, and near-minimizers determine that trace quantitatively. A slicing argument extends the readout to constant coordinate-monomial forms in higher dimension, and a one-cube Whitney construction gives the cubical analogue. A supporting global result computes exact compatible profiles on coherently oriented cubical complexes via an augmented dual network, with a strict interface-loss example; the paper states plainly that these global identities are standard max-flow min-cut and that the box readout is the principal contribution.

